<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Do acoustic panels really help in a normal living room?</h3>
<p>Yes—especially in rooms with lots of hard surfaces. Even a few fabric‑wrapped panels or decorative acoustic art on the wall behind your seating can reduce echo and make voices feel closer. If you rent, look for peel‑and‑stick options or stand‑mounted panels you can remove without damage.</p>

<h3>Is carpet better than hardwood for hearing?</h3>
<p>For speech clarity, carpet (plus a pad) absorbs sound and reduces reflections, which helps. If you love hard floors, add area rugs, runners, and upholstered furniture to get most of the benefit without changing the flooring.</p>

<h3>Are sound machines safe for my hearing?</h3>
<p>Used thoughtfully, yes. Keep the volume as low as needed to cover intrusive sounds, place the device across the room rather than next to your ear, and choose steady pink or brown noise instead of very bright white noise. If you use hearing aids, consult your audiologist about nighttime settings.</p>

<h3>If I optimize my home, do I still need hearing aids?</h3>
<p>Home changes lower background noise and effort, but they can’t replace the boost and clarity hearing aids provide when there’s a hearing loss. Many people find the combination—well‑tuned hearing aids plus a calmer room—feels dramatically easier. An audiologist can help you get both pieces right.</p>


